  "mindmap": `
## Introduction to Exercise Physiology

### Why It Matters

- **Physician crisis**: 85% of US medical schools have no mandatory physical activity or nutrition course
- **Personal habits**: physically active physicians counsel more; medical school habits predict practice
- **Movement is medicine**: frequent breaks from sitting improve vascular function; one workout does not cancel 8 hours of sitting
- **Two scopes**: sport exercise physiology (performance) vs clinical exercise physiology (disease, rehab, quality of life)
- **Population**: 48% of adults worldwide miss activity guidelines

### Energy Systems

- **Phosphagen (ATP-PC)**: 0-10 s, anaerobic, limited by creatine phosphate; CP 4x ATP; Type II fibers hold more
- **Glycolysis**: 10-90 s, anaerobic glucose breakdown, produces lactate, limited by acid-base balance
- **Oxidative phosphorylation**: >90 s, aerobic, CHO/fat/protein, 36-38 ATP per glucose
- **Protein**: not used until ~90 min with depleted stores; also anorexia and starvation
- **Lactate pathway**: lactate dehydrogenase, pyruvate to lactate, pH ~7, rising H+, inhibits glycolysis
- **Metabolic acidosis**: the burning sensation, i.e. peripheral fatigue
- **ATP stores**: only 80-100 g (3 ounces)

### Fuel Selection and Intensity

- **RER**: VCO2/VO2; fat 0.7 (23 O2, 16 CO2), carbohydrate 1.0 (6 O2, 6 CO2)
- **Caloric yield**: CHO 5.05 kcal/L O2, fat 4.73 kcal/L O2; at VO2 1.5 L/min for 30 min = 228 vs 212 kcal
- **Fat-burning zone**: false; higher percentage fat at low intensity, higher total fat and calories at moderate-high
- **EPOC**: excess post-exercise oxygen consumption, the afterburn
- **Bottom line**: caloric deficit and diet outrank substrate choice

### Measuring Intensity

- **Heart rate / heart rate reserve**: easiest and most available
- **Borg RPE**: subjective but validated, correlates with heart rate zones
- **Blood lactate threshold**: finger stick or earlobe sampling
- **Ventilatory threshold**: inflection in ventilation
- **VO2 (gold standard)**: indirect calorimetry, open-circuit spirometry, metabolic cart
- **Ambient air**: 20.93% O2, 0.03% CO2, 79.04% N2
- **Modality**: bike below treadmill; upper-body ergometry lowest
- **Determinants of VO2 max**: cardiac output and a-vO2 difference (main), genetics, fiber type, sex, altitude, age ~1%/year, air quality
- **Fiber plasticity**: Type II can gain oxidative properties; Type I cannot become fast

### Thresholds and Clinical Ceilings

- **Ventilation at rest**: 12-25 breaths per minute
- **Ventilatory threshold**: linearity breaks at ~60% max, 40-50% untrained
- **Anaerobic threshold**: VCO2 crosses VO2, lactate spikes; best predictor of endurance
- **Lactate threshold**: 50-60% VO2 max untrained, 75-85% trained
- **EPO**: raises red cell count and oxygen carrying capacity, indirectly raising lactate threshold
- **Clinical ceilings**: angina and ST depression, desaturation, dyspnea +1 to +4, claudication
- **Rehab principle**: terminate at the ceiling, then train up to it to build collaterals, perfusion, a-vO2 difference

### Cardiac Output

- **Equation**: Q = HR x SV; rest ~70 bpm x ~70 mL = 4-5 L/min; maximal ~20 L/min
- **Factors**: venous return (preload), blood volume, sympathetic nervous system, peripheral resistance (afterload)
- **Preload**: end-diastolic stretch, increases elasticity and chamber size, raises stroke volume
- **Afterload**: resistance to ejection; Valsalva in strength training; hypertension, stiff vessels
- **Eccentric hypertrophy**: volume overload from cardiovascular exercise, chamber larger
- **Concentric hypertrophy**: pressure overload from resistance training, chamber smaller, aortic valve narrowing
- **Decrease stroke volume**: HFrEF, MI, tamponade, hypovolemia, pulmonary embolism, severe aortic stenosis, severe tachycardia
- **HFrEF vs HFpEF**: systolic failure vs impaired diastolic filling; both blunt exercise cardiac output
- **Not exercise**: a sympathetic epinephrine surge (startle, esports) is not continuous exercise

### a-vO2 Difference

- **Definition**: arterial minus venous O2 content, mL O2 per 100 mL blood; ~5 at rest to ~15 in exercise
- **Fick equation**: VO2 = Q x a-vO2 difference
- **Type I fibers**: mitochondria and myoglobin rich, oxidative, large a-vO2 difference in endurance athletes
- **Type II (IIx)**: anaerobic, small contribution
- **Increases**: endurance exercise, anemia, early sepsis, mild-moderate heart failure, peripheral arterial disease
- **Decreases**: severe HFrEF, late sepsis, cyanide and mitochondrial toxins, shock, AV fistulas or shunting, sedation or paralysis

### Environmental Extremes

- **Heat**: Tokyo 86.9 F (30.5 C) with high humidity; core temperature rises, dehydration, VO2 max falls
- **Heat index**: 100 F at 15% humidity feels like 96 F; 86 F at 90% humidity feels like 105 F
- **Heat cascade**: skin blood shunting, decreased stroke volume and cardiac output, higher perceived exertion
- **Cold**: ultramarathon deaths from hypothermia; skip outdoor exercise below 0 F or wind chill -25 F
- **Cold effects**: vasoconstriction, increased afterload, bronchoconstriction from dry air, frost nip
- **Snow shovelling**: vasoconstriction, isometric afterload, Valsalva, viscosity and clot risk, morning cortisol
- **Altitude**: changes above 3,900 ft; air always 20.9% O2, barometric pressure falls
- **Acute altitude**: increased HR and cardiac output, unchanged stroke volume, increased ventilation, alkaline body fluids
- **Chronic altitude**: 10-14 days for red cells, capillarization, mitochondria, a-vO2 difference, fatty acid use; reverts in a month
- **Altitude numbers**: VO2 max down 1.5-3.5% per 1,000 ft above ~5,000 ft; Everest ~29,000 ft with saturation under 70%

### Prescription and Adaptation

- **FITT-VP**: Frequency, Intensity, Time, Type, Volume, Progression
- **Acute responses**: increased HR, stroke volume, cardiac output; systolic BP up, diastolic BP down; deeper faster breathing
- **Chronic adaptations**: lower resting HR, more blood volume and RBC mass, higher capillary density, VO2 max up 15-20%, better ventilatory efficiency
- **Timeline**: neural 2-3 weeks, cardiovascular 3-5 weeks, hypertrophy 4-6 weeks, minimum 6 weeks to judge
- **Muscle and fat**: simultaneous gain and loss possible in beginners, returners, and with adequate protein; not sustainable in trained athletes

### Exercise Testing Guidelines

- **Normal responses**: HR ~10 beats per MET, systolic BP ~10 mmHg per MET, diastolic unchanged, VO2 plateaus while ventilation rises
- **Abnormal**: SBP >250 mmHg or a fall >10 mmHg, DBP >115 mmHg, 4% saturation drop, 1.0 mm downsloping ST depression
- **Relative contraindications**: left main stenosis, moderate valve disease, electrolyte abnormalities, resting BP >200/110, dysrhythmias, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, high-degree AV block, ventricular aneurysm, uncontrolled metabolic or chronic infectious disease
- **Absolute termination**: recent ischemic ECG change or MI within 2 days, unstable angina, uncontrolled dysrhythmias, symptomatic severe aortic stenosis, uncontrolled heart failure, acute pulmonary embolus, acute myocarditis or pericarditis, dissecting aneurysm, acute systemic infection
`
